Practical No: 02
Aim-  To clean and transform raw data using Power Query Editor in Power BI. Use an Excel/CSV 

file named Student_Data.xlsx:  
Step-by-Step Procedure  
Step 1: Import the Data  
Student_ID   Student_Name   Course   Marks   City 
101          Amit           BSc IT   78      Mumbai 
102          Neha           BSc CS   85      Pune 
103          Rahul          BSc IT           Mumbai 
104          Priya          BSc CS   91      Pune 
105          Amit           BSc IT   78      Mumbai 
 
 
1. Open Power BI Desktop.  
2. Select Home → Get Data → Excel.  
3. Select Student_Data.xlsx.  
4. Select the required worksheet.  
5. Click Transform Data.  
The Power Query Editor will open.

Step 2: Remove Unnecessary Columns  
1. Select the column that is not required.  
2. Right-click the column.  
3. Select Remove.  
This helps keep only the useful information.

Step 3: Remove Duplicate Records  
1. Select the relevant columns or the entire table.  
2. Go to Home → Remove Rows → Remove Duplicates.  
3. Power Query removes duplicate records.  
In the example, the duplicate Amit record will be removed.

Step 4: Remove Extra Spaces  
To clean names:  
1. Select the Student_Name column.  
2. Go to Transform → Format → Trim.  
3. Extra spaces before or after names are removed.  
For example:  
“Neha” -> “Neha” 

Step 5: Standardize Text  
To make city names consistent:  
1. Select the City column.  
2. Select Transform → Format → Capitalize Each Word.  
For example: 
pune → Pune  
This makes values consistent. 

Step 6: Handle Missing Values  
The Marks column contains a blank value.  
You can:  
Option 1 – Replace with a value  
1. Select Marks.  
2. Choose Transform → Replace Values.  
3. Replace blank/null values with an appropriate value.  
Option 2 – Remove the record  
1. Select Marks.  
2. Choose Home → Remove Rows → Remove Blank Rows if the entire row is unsuitable.  
For student marks, it is generally better to investigate missing marks rather than  automatically  
assuming a score.

Step 7: Change Data Types  
1. Select Student_ID.  
2. Set its data type to Whole Number.  
3. Select Marks.  
4. Set its data type to Whole Number.  
5. Select text columns such as Student_Name, Course, and City. 6. Set them to Text.  
Correct data types are important for calculations and visualizations.

Step 8: Rename Columns  
1. Right-click a column name.  
2. Select Rename.  
3. Give meaningful names. 
Example:  
Student_ID → Student ID  
Student_Name → Student Name
